{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Lumen gateway: guided code tour",
  "description": "Eleven stops through the gateway code. Run PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py in the terminal to execute each scenario offline.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "pattern": "^# ",
      "title": "Orientation: the repo in one screen",
      "description": "Everything the lab does lives in six small files. Open them in this order and you will have read the whole security story.\n\n- `gateway/server.py`: The front door. Routes, token check, Model Armor calls, RBAC, tool pruning, outbound screening.\n- `gateway/dlp.py`: Masking engine. Pure regex, no network. Easy to read and test.\n- `gateway/audit.py`: One function that emits every audit event.\n- `gateway/session.py`: Firestore conversation memory with an ownership check.\n- `cloudbuild.yaml`: Deploy as candidate at 0% traffic, test it, then promote.\n- `security_alert_policy.json`: The two alert conditions, as code.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- Nothing in gateway/ imports an adapter or a data layer. The tools are plain Python functions returning canned data. That is deliberate: the lab teaches the guardrails, not the data source.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun the offline harness once so you know it works: PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py\n\nIt runs the real gateway code with Model Armor, Gemini and Firestore replaced by small fakes. No cloud calls, no cost, and you can set breakpoints anywhere.\n\n**Further reading:** [The lab on GitHub](https://github.com/lowr3nt/lumen-agent-platform-harness), [Cloud Shell](https://docs.cloud.google.com/shell/docs), [CodeTour extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=vsls-contrib.codetour)"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/server.py",
      "pattern": "^def verify_token",
      "title": "Stop 1: Who is calling? verify_token",
      "description": "Every route that matters declares Depends(verify_token). FastAPI runs it first, so no handler body executes for a bad token. The claims dictionary it returns is the only source of identity for everything after: the sub claim is the principal, the groups claim drives RBAC.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- The algorithm list is pinned to HS256. Passing algorithms explicitly is what stops a caller choosing their own.\n- The issuer is checked. The audience is not (verify_aud is False). Ask what that means for a token minted for a different service.\n- The key is a shared secret. In production this would be an asymmetric key fetched from the identity provider, so the gateway could verify but never mint.\n- A failed token raises 401 and writes no audit event. Anything an attacker does before authenticating is invisible in the security log.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 1. Three bad tokens (forged signature, wrong issuer, expired) all return 401, each with a different jose message, and none writes an audit event.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 1`\n\n**Further reading:** [FastAPI dependencies](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/dependencies/), [JSON Web Tokens](https://jwt.io/introduction)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nIf the audit log is your detection source, what does a password-spraying style attack against /invoke look like in it? (Answer: nothing. That is Exercise D.)"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/server.py",
      "pattern": "^def sanitize_inbound_prompt",
      "title": "Stop 2: Inbound screening and the extractor",
      "description": "Model Armor returns one overall verdict (filter_match_state) and a per-filter breakdown. The gateway blocks on the overall verdict, then asks the breakdown which filters fired so the audit event and the 400 message can name them.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- The token is \"match_state: MATCH_FOUND\", prefix included. A bare MATCH_FOUND is also a substring of NO_MATCH_FOUND, so every filter would look triggered on every block. This is Exercise A.\n- The check is on str(result) because the filters are protobuf messages and the sensitive data filter nests its match_state inside inspect_result. One string test covers both shapes.\n- If the breakdown yields nothing, the code falls back to pi_and_jailbreak. A defensive default that can also mislabel an event.\n- Block returns 400, and the response body names the filter. Consider what that tells an attacker.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 2. A jailbreak names pi_and_jailbreak. The lab sample card 4532-8921-3312-9011 passes (it fails the Luhn check, so Model Armor ignores it, and only the gateway DLP masks it later). A Luhn-valid card is blocked and names sdp.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 2`\n\n**Further reading:** [Model Armor overview](https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/overview), [Sanitize prompts and responses](https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/sanitize-prompts-responses), [Sensitive Data Protection](https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs), [The Luhn algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luhn_algorithm)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nThe sample card in the lab data is not a valid card number. Which control still protects it, and at which boundary?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/server.py",
      "pattern": "^def invoke_tool",
      "title": "Stop 3: The deterministic door, /invoke",
      "description": "This is the direct API door. Authorization is a dictionary lookup and a set intersection. No model is involved, so the answer is the same every time. There are two denial statuses: tool not in the registry, and tool registered but caller lacks the group.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- Order matters: authorize first, screen second, execute third, mask fourth. A caller without rights never costs a Model Armor call.\n- Parameters are screened only if they are top-level strings. A string nested in a dict or list is never screened. This is Exercise F.\n- The if/elif ladder that calls the tool maps parameter names by hand. Fine for three tools, a trap for thirty.\n- The tool result is turned into a JSON string, masked, screened, then parsed back into JSON. Remember that when you reach Stop 6.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 3. Alex (marketing) asking for lookup_customer_record gets 403 DENIED_INSUFFICIENT_PRIVILEGES. A made-up tool gets 403 DENIED_UNREGISTERED_TOOL.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 3`\n\n**Further reading:** [Sanitize prompts and responses](https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/sanitize-prompts-responses), [FastAPI dependencies](https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/dependencies/)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWhy does the unregistered-tool check come before the group check?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/server.py",
      "pattern": "^def agent_chat",
      "title": "Stop 4: The model door, /agent/chat",
      "description": "Here authorization is by omission. The model is only handed the tools the caller may use, so a marketing analyst cannot get the model to call lookup_customer_record because the model has never heard of it. The wrapper exists so every tool call the model makes is audited, with invocation_mode set to chat_agent so you can tell it from a direct API call.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- @functools.wraps copies the original function signature onto the wrapper. The Gemini SDK reads the signature to build the tool schema. Without it the model is told the tools take no parameters (Exercise B). This was a real bug in this lab and the reason a promotion gate once failed.\n- The factory function is called inside the request handler. It captures user_email and user_groups from the enclosing scope as closure variables. That is why it is nested, and why hoisting it is a stretch exercise.\n- The wrapper does not check authorization. Pruning is the only control on this path. If the list were ever built wrongly, nothing inside the wrapper would stop a call.\n- The order is: screen inbound, prune tools, load history, log session start, call model, screen outbound, save history.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 4: Alex is exposed one tool, Dana three. Run scenario 5: the parameters the SDK derived for each tool. Then do Exercise B and run it again.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 4 5`\n\n**Further reading:** [functools.wraps](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functools.html#functools.wraps), [Function calling with Gemini](https://docs.cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/multimodal/function-calling)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nThe model door and the direct door enforce RBAC in different ways. Which fails closed if the code has a bug, and which fails open?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/session.py",
      "pattern": "^def get_session_history",
      "title": "Stop 5: Conversation memory and ownership",
      "description": "History lives in Firestore, one document per session id. The session id comes from the caller (X-Session-Id header), so ownership has to be checked: otherwise guessing a session id would read someone else's conversation. agent_chat catches PermissionError and turns it into a 403.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- MAX_HISTORY_TURNS is 10, and the window keeps the last 20 messages. Bounded history limits cost and limits how far a poisoned early turn can persist.\n- append_session_turns reads, modifies and writes without a transaction. Two simultaneous requests on one session can lose a turn.\n- PROJECT_ID is hard-coded in this file, while server.py reads it from the environment. Fork this lab into another project and sessions still go to the original.\n- The default session id is session-adhoc-<email>, so a caller with no header always resumes their own ad hoc session.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 7: two turns in one session, then a different principal asks for the same session id and gets 403.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 7`\n\n**Further reading:** [Firestore (Native mode)](https://docs.cloud.google.com/firestore/native/docs)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWhat would you change to make the history write safe under concurrent requests?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/dlp.py",
      "pattern": "^def mask_text",
      "title": "Stop 6: The masking engine",
      "description": "Two tiers. Cards and SSNs are masked for everyone, administrators included. Emails and phones are masked for everyone except Lumen-Data-Admins. redact_sensitive_payload walks any nested structure and applies mask_text to every string, counting redactions, and emits one SECURITY_PII_REDACTED event if the count is above zero.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- Pattern order matters. Each substitution runs on the output of the one before. A new pattern placed after the phone pattern can be partly eaten by it (Exercise C).\n- _walk uses nonlocal to accumulate one counter across the whole structure. That is why a single event covers a whole payload.\n- PATTERN_PHONE has no leading \\b, so it can bite into the middle of longer digit runs. Regex DLP is a floor, not a guarantee.\n- Only values are masked. Dictionary keys are not.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 6, first half: Alex gets three redactions, Dana two, from the same record.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 6`\n\n**Further reading:** [Python re module](https://docs.python.org/3/library/re.html), [Sensitive Data Protection](https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWhich is worse for a masking engine, a false positive or a false negative? Does your answer change by data type?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/server.py",
      "pattern": "^def sanitize_outbound_response",
      "title": "Stop 7: Egress, mask first then screen",
      "description": "Every reply, whether a tool result on /invoke or model text on /agent/chat, goes through the same function. DLP runs before Model Armor so that sensitive data is gone before it reaches any other service, and so Model Armor judges what the caller would actually receive.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- A blocked response is a 502, not a 400. The fault is the upstream result, not the caller's request.\n- On /invoke the raw output is serialized to a JSON string first, so DLP receives one string and the dictionary walker never descends into it. Masking still works because the regexes run on the whole string. This is why the walker looks more important than it is on this path.\n- The function returns str, so the chat path returns text and the invoke path parses it back into JSON.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun scenario 6, second half: the same record with DLP in place returns 200 masked. With DLP bypassed, Model Armor sees the raw card and the gateway returns 502.\n\nRun in the terminal: `PYTHONPATH=. python scripts/offline_tour.py 6`\n\n**Further reading:** [Sanitize prompts and responses](https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/sanitize-prompts-responses), [Model Armor templates](https://docs.cloud.google.com/model-armor/manage-templates)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nIf you swapped the order, screen then mask, what changes for the data, for the 502 rate and for the audit log?"
    },
    {
      "file": "gateway/audit.py",
      "pattern": "^def log_audit_event",
      "title": "Stop 8: One function, every event",
      "description": "All six event types go through here. The K_SERVICE guard means the Cloud Logging client is only created when running on Cloud Run, which is why tests and make check run anywhere without credentials.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- Each event is emitted twice on Cloud Run: once as a structured log entry, once as a printed line that Cloud Run captures from stdout. That is why log queries need the logName line and why the log-based metrics filter on both resource type and log name.\n- Cloud Logging failures are caught and logged, never raised. An outage in the logging pipeline never takes down the gateway. Decide whether that is right for a security log.\n- Severity is chosen by the caller of this function, not by a table. Alerts keyed on severity would drift. Alerts here key on event_type for that reason.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRun grep -rn \"event_type=\" gateway/ and match every call site to one of the six event types.\n\n**Further reading:** [Cloud Run logging](https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/logging), [Logging query language](https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logging-query-language)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWhat is missing from the six event types if you wanted to detect credential stuffing?"
    },
    {
      "file": "cloudbuild.yaml",
      "pattern": "id: 'deploy-candidate'",
      "title": "Stop 9: The deploy gate",
      "description": "A new revision is deployed with no traffic and a tag, so it gets its own URL. The tests run against that URL. Only if they all pass does the last step move 100% of traffic to the candidate. A failing test stops the build and users never see the revision.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- The candidate URL is hard-coded in the YAML. Fork the lab to a new project and the gate tests the wrong service, or none.\n- The tests mint their own tokens with the public shared secret. That is fine for a lab and a bad pattern anywhere else.\n- The multi-turn test once asserted on model wording, then it failed when the model phrased things differently. A gate should assert on facts (status codes, event types, tool lists), not prose.\n\n**Try it**\n\nRead scripts/test_multiturn.py and find what it asserts. Ask whether each assertion is a fact or a phrase.\n\n**Further reading:** [Cloud Build](https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs), [Cloud Run traffic and rollbacks](https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWhat is the smallest change that stops a bad revision from ever being promoted, even if the tests are wrong?"
    },
    {
      "file": "security_alert_policy.json",
      "pattern": "RBAC Denials Detected",
      "title": "Stop 10: Detection as code",
      "description": "One alert policy, two conditions. Injections alert on any event. RBAC denials alert on a rate. With a 300 second window, 0.008 per second is 2.4 events, so three denials in five minutes trips it and two does not.\n\n**What to notice**\n\n- ALIGN_RATE converts a count to a per-second rate, so the threshold is a rate, not a count. The displayName says \"> 2 events\" and the arithmetic is the real contract.\n- The policy has no notification channels, so it raises an incident but pages nobody. It alerts, it does not page.\n- The metrics are per Cloud Run revision, so a new deploy starts a fresh series.\n\n**Try it**\n\nCompute by hand how many denials in 10 minutes it takes if the alignment period were 600s and the threshold unchanged.\n\n**Further reading:** [Alerting overview](https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/alerts), [Log-based metrics](https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics)\n\n**Question for the group**\n\nWho should be notified when this fires, and what should they be able to do in the first five minutes?"
    }
  ]
}